Boiling occurs when the vapor pressure of the liquid equals the atmospheric pressure, which means that the temperature is equal to the boiling point of the substance. Given the value of $\Delta H_{\text {vaporization }}$ and the normal boiling point of water, we can calculate the boiling point at sea level. Then, we subtract the atmospheric pressure difference due to altitude to get the boiling point on the top of Mt. Everest.

The boiling point of water at standard atmospheric pressure is $373.12 \mathrm{~K}$. Subtracting the atmospheric pressure difference of 253 Torr from this gives:

$$
373.12 - 253 = 120.12 \mathrm{~K}
$$

Now, convert this to Kelvin for consistency:

$$
120.12 + 273.15 = 393.27 \mathrm{~K}
$$

Therefore, the boiling point of water on the top of Mt. Everest is approximately $393.27 \mathrm{~K}$, not any of the options provided. The answer is (X), since it doesn't match any of the given options.
